<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    RDR-169 Phase B, Gap 1 (bead nexus-zw2em) - retention TEXT NOT NULL
    DEFAULT 'full' CHECK (retention IN ('reference-only','full')) on
    nexus.chunks, plus ALTER COLUMN chunk_text DROP NOT NULL: the schema
    slice that lets a chunk row carry identity (address + span + chash +
    metadata + embedding) without content, for a reference-only consumer
    (Conductus / any docuverse client) that owns the bytes elsewhere.

    RE-TARGETED FROM chunks_&lt;dim&gt; TO nexus.chunks (session nexus-70,
    Sam: 'do it', 2026-09-11): RDR-169's own Technical Design section
    predates RDR-191 Phase 4 (vectors-004-unify-chunks.xml), which collapsed
    chunks_384/768/1024 into ONE nexus.chunks table with three nullable
    typed embedding columns (embedding_384/768/1024, exactly_one_embedding
    CHECK). Every "per chunks_&lt;dim&gt;" instruction in the RDR now applies
    to nexus.chunks alone - there is only one table to ALTER, not three.
    (RDR-169's cross-repo conexus etl_t3.py pairing is separately moot:
    conexus-od6a records the T3 ETL as the retired migration path, so this
    is engine-only with no paired client-repo edit - unlike memory-003's
    contemporaneous client pairing, which genuinely needed one.)

    chunk_tsv GENERATED EXPRESSION IS UNCHANGED (CA-2, verified by schema
    spike, RDR-169 Research Findings): nexus.chunks.chunk_tsv is
    `GENERATED ALWAYS AS (to_tsvector('english', chunk_text)) STORED` with
    no NOT NULL of its own (vectors-004-unify-chunks.xml). Postgres
    evaluates `to_tsvector('english', NULL)` to a NULL tsvector, which the
    `@@` match operator and the GIN index (idx_chunks_tsv) both treat as a
    non-match - a reference-only row is excluded from every FTS/hybrid
    candidate path (vectors-007's hybrid_search_&lt;dim&gt;: `chunk_tsv @@
    plainto_tsquery(...) OR p_query_text &lt;% chunk_text` - both operands
    NULL, both false) by construction, with zero edit to the generated
    column itself. The earlier COALESCE(chunk_text,'') idea (RDR-169
    Alternatives) is unnecessary: empty and NULL tsvector both exclude.

    embedding_384/768/1024 stay nullable-per-column / exactly_one_embedding
    CHECK unchanged (a reference-only chunk is still a vector - G4's
    embed-without-store route stores a caller-supplied precomputed
    embedding, never a NULL one); tenant_id stays NOT NULL (RDR-169 binding
    constraint 3: RLS is unconditional, content nullability is orthogonal
    to tenancy). RLS itself (ENABLE/FORCE + tenant_isolation policy,
    vectors-004 step 3) is untouched - this changeset is a bare ADD
    COLUMN + ALTER COLUMN, no DML, so the FORCE-RLS migration-role DML
    no-op class (nexus-1wjmq) does not apply here (see
    tests/test_changelog_rls_lint.py's own scope note: only DML against a
    FORCE table is in-scope for that lint).

    Re-index / retention-transition semantics (RDR-169 Technical Design):
    a full -> reference-only transition is PROHIBITED at the application
    layer (PgVectorRepository#upsertReferenceOnlyChunk's pre-INSERT guard
    SELECT, nexus-xvb6b). The GUARD is still application-level (a DB CHECK
    cannot express "reject this specific transition, but allow the row to
    already be in the target state") - but the STATIC invariant the guard
    relies on, "chunk_text IS NULL iff retention='reference-only'", IS now
    a schema-level biconditional CHECK (chunks_content_retention_consistent,
    fix round 1, T2 critique-nexus-zw2em-rdr169-phase-b-2026-09-11): defense-
    in-depth against any FUTURE write path that might not honour the two
    existing paths' own discipline. REVISED RATIONALE (corrects this file's
    original text, which claimed a two-column CHECK "would also have to
    tolerate a legitimate transient ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE state" - traced
    both write paths and that transient state never occurs: Postgres
    evaluates a CHECK constraint against the FINAL post-statement row, not
    incrementally mid-UPDATE, and neither upsertChunksInternal's ordinary
    DO UPDATE (always pairs non-null chunk_text with retention='full',
    fix round 1's own added `.set(ch.retention(), "full")`) nor
    upsertReferenceOnlyChunk's DO UPDATE (never touches chunk_text, always
    binds retention='reference-only') ever asks Postgres to accept a row
    where the two columns disagree, transiently or otherwise). The ONE-WAY
    full->reference-only prohibition itself remains app-level, because the
    biconditional alone cannot distinguish "this chash never had content"
    from "this chash HAD content and is being silently NULLed" - both are
    chunk_text=NULL + retention='reference-only', which the CHECK accepts
    either way; only the guard's pre-INSERT SELECT (which reads the ROW
    THAT WOULD BE OVERWRITTEN) can tell them apart.

    Placed after tuples-002-sweep-indexes.xml (current tail) and before the
    grants-nexus-svc.xml runAlways block, matching the established
    late-append convention (D0.1; see vectors-013's / memory-003's own
    &lt;include&gt; comments for the identical reasoning).

    D0.6 rollback: drops the CHECK and the column, restores chunk_text NOT
    NULL. A reference-only row (chunk_text NULL) cannot survive that
    restoration - stated inline, not silently omitted, matching
    memory-003-ttl-days.xml's identical rollback discipline: any
    reference-only row present at rollback time is UNREPRESENTABLE under
    the pre-migration shape and the ALTER COLUMN ... SET NOT NULL will
    fail loud (not silently coerce) if one exists. This is deliberate: a
    rollback that silently deleted or NULL-coerced live docuverse data
    would be worse than a rollback that refuses. The biconditional CHECK
    is now added across TWO changesets (vectors-014-1: ADD CONSTRAINT ...
    NOT VALID; vectors-014-2: VALIDATE CONSTRAINT), an unreleased revision
    to avoid an ACCESS EXCLUSIVE full-table scan on deploy (see
    vectors-014-1's own comment) - rollback still drops the SAME one
    constraint, just with vectors-014-2's rollback owning the DROP (it
    runs first, in reverse-chronological order) and vectors-014-1's
    rollback no longer repeating it.
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <changeSet id="vectors-014-1" author="nexus-zw2em">
        <comment>
            RDR-169 Phase B Gap 1: nexus.chunks gains retention TEXT NOT NULL
            DEFAULT 'full' CHECK (retention IN ('reference-only','full'));
            chunk_text DROP NOT NULL; and (fix round 1) the biconditional
            chunks_content_retention_consistent CHECK ((chunk_text IS NULL) =
            (retention = 'reference-only')), defense-in-depth against any
            future write path bypassing the two existing paths' own
            discipline. chunk_tsv generated expression, exactly_one_embedding
            CHECK, tenant_id NOT NULL, and RLS are all unchanged (see file
            header). The biconditional CHECK is added NOT VALID (conexus
            deploy-side directive, unreleased): plain ADD CONSTRAINT takes
            ACCESS EXCLUSIVE and full-scans nexus.chunks to verify every
            existing row before the lock is even granted to release -
            ~440k rows in production, an outage-shaped lock hold for what
            is otherwise a routine additive migration. NOT VALID skips that
            scan (only new/updated rows are checked going forward); the
            following changeset (vectors-014-2) validates the existing data
            separately, under SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE, which does not block
            ordinary reads/writes the way ACCESS EXCLUSIVE does.
        </comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.chunks
    ADD COLUMN retention TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT 'full'
    CONSTRAINT chunks_retention_chk CHECK (retention IN ('reference-only', 'full'));

ALTER TABLE nexus.chunks
    ALTER COLUMN chunk_text DROP NOT NULL;

ALTER TABLE nexus.chunks
    ADD CONSTRAINT chunks_content_retention_consistent
    CHECK ((chunk_text IS NULL) = (retention = 'reference-only')) NOT VALID;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <!-- chunks_content_retention_consistent is dropped by vectors-014-2's own
                 rollback (which runs FIRST in reverse-chronological order, since it is
                 the LATER changeset) - this rollback no longer repeats that DROP so the
                 pair rolls back cleanly with each changeset undoing exactly what it
                 added, rather than the constraint being torn down twice. -->
            <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.chunks
    ALTER COLUMN chunk_text SET NOT NULL;

ALTER TABLE nexus.chunks
    DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS chunks_retention_chk;

ALTER TABLE nexus.chunks
    DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS retention;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="vectors-014-2" author="nexus-zw2em">
        <comment>
            Validates chunks_content_retention_consistent (added NOT VALID by
            vectors-014-1) against the existing rows. ALTER TABLE ... VALIDATE
            CONSTRAINT takes SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE (not ACCESS EXCLUSIVE) and
            only needs a single pass over the table with no row-level locking
            of concurrent writers - the standard two-step pattern for adding
            a CHECK constraint to a large, live table without an availability
            hit (conexus deploy-side directive, unreleased: without this
            split, ADD CONSTRAINT alone was measured to full-scan
            nexus.chunks (~440k rows in production) under ACCESS EXCLUSIVE).
        </comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.chunks
    VALIDATE CONSTRAINT chunks_content_retention_consistent;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.chunks
    DROP CONSTRAINT IF EXISTS chunks_content_retention_consistent;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
